Looking at the error, the issue is that Dafny cannot automatically prove the assertion `sum(a[..i+1]) == sum(a[..i]) + a[i]`. This is because Dafny's automatic induction doesn't always work well with sequence slicing. We need to provide a lemma to help Dafny prove this property.

Here's the corrected code:

```dafny
function sum(a: seq<int>): int
  requires |a| >= 0
{
  if |a| == 0 then 0 else a[0] + sum(a[1..])
}

lemma sum_append(a: seq<int>, i: int)
  requires 0 <= i < |a|
  ensures sum(a[..i+1]) == sum(a[..i]) + a[i]
{
  if i == 0 {
    assert a[..1] == [a[0]];
    assert a[..0] == [];
  } else {
    calc {
      sum(a[..i+1]);
      a[0] + sum(a[1..i+1]);
      a[0] + sum(a[1..i]) + a[i];
      sum(a[..i]) + a[i];
    }
  }
}

method cumsum(a: seq<int>) returns (b: seq<int>)
  requires |a| >= 0
  ensures |b| == |a|
  ensures forall i :: 0 <= i < |a| ==> b[i] == sum(a[..i+1])
{
  b := [];
  var i := 0;
  while i < |a|
    invariant 0 <= i <= |a|
    invariant |b| == i
    invariant forall j :: 0 <= j < i ==> b[j] == sum(a[..j+1])
  {
    if i == 0 {
      b := b + [a[i]];
    } else {
      sum_append(a, i);
      var next_val := b[i-1] + a[i];
      assert next_val == sum(a[..i+1]) by {
        assert b[i-1] == sum(a[..i]);
        assert sum(a[..i+1]) == sum(a[..i]) + a[i];
      }
      b := b + [next_val];
    }
    i := i + 1;
  }
}
```

The key changes:
1. Added a lemma `sum_append` that proves `sum(a[..i+1]) == sum(a[..i]) + a[i]` using induction
2. Called the lemma before using the assertion in the loop body
3. The lemma uses `calc` to show the step-by-step reasoning

This should allow Dafny to verify the program successfully.
