We need to implement an efficient, non-recursive algorithm with proper preconditions and postconditions.  

A common task that fits this description is **finding the maximum element in an array**.  
We'll write a Dafny program that:  

- Works for arrays of integers (can be extended to other types).  
- Uses a loop instead of recursion.  
- Has proper preconditions (e.g., array non-null).  
- Has postconditions ensuring correctness.  
- Handles empty arrays by returning a sentinel value or using a precondition.  

Here’s the Dafny code:

```dafny
method FindMax(a: array<int>) returns (max: int)
    requires a.Length > 0
    ensures forall i :: 0 <= i < a.Length ==> a[i] <= max
    ensures exists i :: 0 <= i < a.Length && a[i] == max
{
    max := a[0];
    var i: int := 1;
    while i < a.Length
        invariant 1 <= i <= a.Length
        invariant forall j :: 0 <= j < i ==> a[j] <= max
        invariant exists j :: 0 <= j < i && a[j] == max
    {
        if a[i] > max {
            max := a[i];
        }
        i := i + 1;
    }
}
```

This program:  
- Requires the array to have at least one element.  
- Guarantees that the returned value is the maximum.  
- Uses a loop, not recursion.  
- Is efficient (linear time).  
- Is easy to understand and maintain.
